In recent years, Nigeria has grappled with escalating security challenges, particularly in the form of the farmers-herdsmen conflict, which has had profound implications for political stability and food security in the country. The conflict, characterized by clashes between sedentary farmers and nomadic herdsmen over access to land and resources, has led to widespread violence, displacement, and economic disruption, particularly in the Southeast region. Other factors include the nature if the Nigeria’s borders which are porous, overconcentration of infrastructure in urban centers, ineffective security response and lack of political will to address the issue. This study is guided by tow research questions: (1)Does the internal displacements of crop farmers undermine food production in Southeast Nigeria? (2)Does the incessant killing of indigenous crop farmers undermine food affordability in Southeast Nigeria? The research examined two hypotheses: (1)The internal displacements of crop farmers undermined food production in the southeast Region and (2) the incessant killing of indigenous crop farmers undermined food affordability in Southeast. This is qualitative research which makes use of expo facto research design. Data was collected through documentary method and was analyzed using content analysis and eco violence theory. Findings show that the socio-economic effects of farmers – herders’ conflict in Nigeria leads to dire productivity and agricultural consequences that causes food shortages and food insecurity in the country. We recommended that government should ensure and encourage creating of farms and ranch system for livestock in Nigeria.&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp; 
<div>
 Keywords: Farmers-Herdsmen Conflict, Food Security, Resource Competition, Agricultural Disruption, and Southeast Nigeria&nbsp;&nbsp;
</div>

Background. Decisions about health investment require evidence on health outcomes, the costs of health programmes, and all downstream outputs including potential gains in gross domestic product (GDP). This study extends to a broader country group the production framework developed in work informing the 2018 World Health Organization (WHO) Investment Case and a published 2019 ten-country analysis estimating the GDP gains resulting from reaching Universal Health Coverage. A companion paper to this one presents an audit and reassessment of the 2019 implementation. The present study addresses the question of how a common spendingto-health response might change GDP across a wider set of countries.&nbsp; 
<div>
 Methods. Simulations were attempted for the 67 countries included in a published WHO resource-needs analysis, 26 additional low-income and lower-middle-income economies, and 21 data-ready upper-middleincome economies under the World Bank fiscal-year 2027 classification. The mortality response, calculated from published programme-specific costs and postbirth deaths averted, was 88.712 deaths per additional million 2014 US dollars. Direct disability benefits were anchored to an archived ten-country reconstruction of the WHO Economic Projections for Illness and Cost of Treatment (EPIC) framework at 292.507 years lived with disability averted per million dollars. Responses were assumed to be linear and constrained by countries' burden ceilings. All 101 countries (54 original + 47 additional) received a sustained annual spending increment equal to 10% of baseline health expenditure for five years, with the full increment assigned to the six-category programme envelope studied in the 2017 resource needs analysis; however, the ten reconstructed countries from the published 2019 economic-impact analysis retained their archived spending paths. Shared health-system costs were excluded. Six of the 26 additional low-income and lower-middle-income economies required imputed capital inputs.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Results. Simulations were completed for 64 of 67 countries from the published resource-needs analysis and 47 additional economies, for a total of 111 country simulations.&nbsp;Five-year additional GDP was $630.92 billion against $460.065 billion of additional spending in the first group and $86.097 billion against $54.356 billion in the harmonised additional-country extension. The corresponding gains were 0.3768% and 1.1977% of cumulative baseline GDP. Across all 111 completed scenarios, additional GDP was $717.017 billion against $514.421 billion of spending. Twelve countries reached eligible mortality ceilings; none reached its disability ceiling. Three economies from the resource-needs analysis in 2017 were excluded because required inputs were unavailable.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 <span>Conclusions. The scenarios indicate positive production effects under the specified assumptions, but do not strongly suggest fiscal self-financing in any arbitrary country or country group. Results remain conditional on programme-only costs, transported health responses, assumed disability-to-labour conversion and simplified macroeconomic dynamics.</span>
</div>
